  "magnitude_instructions": "As a professional anthropologist, you must demonstrate your understanding of the Numerons’ number language. Each block contains 20 magnitude comparisons in a random order. To move on to the next phase, you must answer at least 18 out of 20 correctly (90% accuracy). If you score below 90%, you will repeat the same 20 comparisons in a new random order. Please do not take notes or use other aids; try to memorize the relative magnitudes instead.\n\nOn the next pages, you will see statements such as “Lod > Zok.” Press J for Correct or K for Incorrect. Make each judgment as quickly and accurately as you can.\n\nStudy the relative magnitudes of the Numerons’ words—for example, Lod is greater than Zok. When you have memorized the relative magnitudes of the Powers, press Right Key to continue.",
  "task_pages": [
    {
      "body": "Starting on the next page, you will see one number expression at a time. Your job is to decide whether it is a real number word in the Numerons’ language. In your own native language, for example, not all combinations of number words when placed together form another number word. If X is a number word and Y is a number word, the combinations XX, YY, XY, and YX may or may not be number words. Only certain combinations are real number words.",
      "notes": ""
    },
    {
      "body": "Please remember to study them carefully and then decide if they are a real number word. \n\nPress J for Yes or K for No. Respond as quickly as you can while trying to be correct.\n\nThe first trial starts in the next page.",
      "notes": ""
    }
  ],
